issues.recommendation:
Ship it as a separate public repo `pranavnandyalam/robot-ci` (root action.yml, Marketplace-listable):
- Each release pins topple-sim and the image digest; `v1` moves only with compatible releases; the README shows SHA pinning.
- The action never comments. It writes outputs, the job summary (same Markdown as the PR comment, minus the GIF), annotations and an artifact. Offer `robot-ci/report` (subfolder) as the optional workflow_run half.
- All logic lives in a tested CLI, `topple ci --github-actions`: reads INPUT_*, validates the robot first, runs the eval, writes GITHUB_OUTPUT and GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY, and exits 0 on pass, 1 on a failed gate, 2 on invalid input or robot.
- The child gets an allow-listed environment only: PATH, HOME=<tmp>, LANG=C.UTF-8, OPENBLAS/OMP/MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2. Never ACTIONS_* or GITHUB_TOKEN.
- Document that subprocess isolation is not a boundary against hostile fork PRs in your own repo; use container there.

action.yml:
```yaml
name: Topple Robot CI
description: Score a robot controller in MuJoCo on every push - deterministic score, replay GIF, job summary.
branding: {icon: activity, color: blue}
inputs:
  controller: {description: 'Controller .py file, or a directory with controller.py plus assets.', required: true}
  protocol: {description: 'Protocol id, e.g. balance-v1/standard (alias eval_v1) or balance-v1/clean.', default: balance-v1/standard}
  robot: {description: 'Optional directory with robot.xml + params.yaml (+ meshes); validated against balancer.robot.v1; results labeled custom, never ranked.', default: ''}
  isolation: {description: 'subprocess (any runner) or container (Linux + Docker, same image as the official scorer).', default: subprocess}
  episodes: {description: 'public = every public seed; N = first N seeds (smoke, unrankable).', default: public}
  min-score: {description: 'Fail when the protocol score is below this (0-100); empty = report only.', default: ''}
  max-errors: {description: 'Fail when more episodes than this end with an error.', default: '0'}
  compare-to: {description: 'Optional previous results.json for deltas.', default: ''}
  video: {description: 'highlight | worst | all | none', default: highlight}
  summary: {description: 'Write the report to the job summary.', default: 'true'}
  artifact-name: {description: 'Artifact for results.json, trajectories, videos; empty = skip.', default: topple-results}
  topple-version: {description: 'topple-sim version; defaults to the one this release was tested with.', default: 0.3.0}
outputs:
  score: {description: 'Protocol score 0-100, 2 decimals.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.score }}'}
  success-rate: {description: 'Fraction of successful episodes.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.success-rate }}'}
  errors: {description: 'Episodes that ended with an error.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.errors }}'}
  passed: {description: 'true when min-score and max-errors pass.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.passed }}'}
  delta: {description: 'score minus compare-to score.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.delta }}'}
  rankable: {description: 'true only for the official robot in container isolation.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.rankable }}'}
  robot-problems: {description: 'JSON array of balancer.robot.v1 violations.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.robot-problems }}'}
  results-path: {description: 'Path to results.json.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.results-path }}'}
  video-path: {description: 'Path to the highlight GIF.', value: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.video-path }}'}
runs:
  using: composite
  steps:
    - name: Check runner
      if: inputs.isolation == 'container' && runner.os != 'Linux'
      shell: bash
      run: echo '::error title=Topple::isolation=container needs runs-on ubuntu-24.04 (Linux + Docker)'; exit 1
    - uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@<sha> # v10.x
      with: {version: 0.13.0, enable-cache: true, cache-suffix: 'topple-${{ inputs.topple-version }}'}
    - name: Headless OpenGL
      if: runner.os == 'Linux' && inputs.video != 'none'
      shell: bash
      run: sudo apt-get install -y -qq --no-install-recommends libosmesa6 || { sudo apt-get update -qq && sudo apt-get install -y -qq --no-install-recommends libosmesa6; }
    - id: topple
      shell: bash
      env: {TOPPLE_VERSION: '${{ inputs.topple-version }}', INPUT_CONTROLLER: '${{ inputs.controller }}', INPUT_PROTOCOL: '${{ inputs.protocol }}', INPUT_ROBOT: '${{ inputs.robot }}', INPUT_ISOLATION: '${{ inputs.isolation }}', INPUT_EPISODES: '${{ inputs.episodes }}', INPUT_MIN_SCORE: '${{ inputs.min-score }}', INPUT_MAX_ERRORS: '${{ inputs.max-errors }}', INPUT_COMPARE_TO: '${{ inputs.compare-to }}', INPUT_VIDEO: '${{ inputs.video }}', INPUT_SUMMARY: '${{ inputs.summary }}'}
      run: uvx --from "topple-sim==$TOPPLE_VERSION" topple ci --github-actions
    - if: always() && inputs.artifact-name != ''
      uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha>
      with: {name: '${{ inputs.artifact-name }}', path: '${{ steps.topple.outputs.out-dir }}', retention-days: 14, if-no-files-found: warn}
```

Caller usage:
```yaml
- uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7, persist-credentials: false
- uses: pranavnandyalam/robot-ci@v1
  with: {controller: controllers/balancer.py, robot: robot/, min-score: 70}
```

Estimated runtime (Linux, warm cache): setup-uv ~4 s + OSMesa ~10 s (only when video != none) + uvx install ~3-10 s + 20 episodes ~5-10 s + highlight ~2-5 s ≈ 25-40 s.

Make rendering an optional extra, `topple-sim[render]` (imageio, imageio-ffmpeg ~30 MB), so `video: none` installs and the eval image stay small. Also verify how setup-uv builds its cache key when the caller's repo has no uv.lock.

issues.problem:
verify_episode only shows that qpos is what scenario + ctrl produce. Nothing ties ctrl to the submitted controller.py.

eval.yml runs on `pull_request`, so the PR's own copies of eval.yml, python/topple and uv.lock execute. Every artifact it produces is attacker-authored. A fork can:
- upload a ctrl sequence from an offline optimizer, or from an oracle that saw ground truth and the future pushes, next to a dummy controller.py; it re-simulates bit-exactly and gets ranked;
- set `isolation: "container"` in results.json, so `EvalResults.rankable` is meaningless for artifacts;
- add any workflow named `eval`, which also triggers publish.yml.

"Reported scores are ignored; re-verify and re-score" therefore does not stop score tampering. It only stops physically impossible tampering.

issues.recommendation:
Take ranked numbers only from a run whose harness is trusted.

In publish.yml (workflow_run, so the default-branch workflow and topple are used), add a `score` job:
- read-only permissions only (`contents: read`, plus `packages: read` if the image is private), no secrets, no `actions/cache` or setup-uv cache;
- fetch the submission files as data at `github.event.workflow_run.head_sha`, via `gh api repos/$REPO/contents/<path>?ref=$HEAD_SHA` or by fetching refs/pull/N/head;
- never check out and install PR code (checkout v7 refuses it in workflow_run anyway);
- run the digest-pinned image built from main with the sandbox in the uid-separation issue;
- emit results and trajectories as job outputs or artifacts.

A separate `comment` job (`actions: read`, `pull-requests: write`) only reads them.

Beyond that:
- eval.yml becomes optional fast feedback, labelled "preview, unverified".
- Decide rankability by which job produced the run, never by the `isolation` field.
- Use the same trusted path for nightly top-k.
- Keep open-loop replay as an integrity check on the stored traj.npz that the GIF and Arena use.

issues.problem:
With `--user 65534`, the host also runs as 65534. Without CAP_SETUID it can't start the child under another uid, and without CAP_KILL it couldn't signal one. So host and child share a uid, which means the child can:
- SIGKILL or SIGSTOP the host;
- read /proc/<host>/environ and reopen /proc/<host>/fd/* (Yama only restricts ATTACH);
- write the host's output directory, or leave a setsid()'d grandchild that rewrites results.json between the host's write and container exit.

/proc/<pid>/cmdline is world-readable across uids, so the salt must never be in argv.

This doesn't matter for eval.yml, whose harness is untrusted anyway. It is the entire integrity story for the trusted re-run and for the nightly secret salt ("seeds unreadable to the child").

Worst cases allowed by the stated per-call limits:
- act just under 0.25 s × 1000 steps × 20 episodes ≈ 83 min;
- load just under 30 s × 20 episodes ≈ 10 min;
- episode CPU 30 s × 20 episodes = 10 min.

Each hits timeout-minutes 10: the job is cancelled with no artifacts and no PR comment. That fails the done-when and the "sleeping to stall the host" red-team case.

On macOS, a child that ignores SIGXCPU survives RLIMIT_CPU with soft == hard. Linux sends SIGKILL at the hard limit.

issues.recommendation:
Launch with `Popen([sys.executable, '-s', '-P', '-B', '-X', 'utf8', '-m', <child entry>, ...], env=MIN_ENV)`. MIN_ENV contains only:
- PYTHONHASHSEED=0 and PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1;
- OMP_NUM_THREADS, OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS, MKL_NUM_THREADS, NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS and VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS all =1;
- HOME and TMPDIR = the episode directory;
- LANG and LC_ALL = C.UTF-8; PATH=/usr/bin:/bin;
- the CPU-feature pins from the cross-hardware issue.

Never use preexec_fn: it is unsafe once the host has OpenBLAS threads. Use Popen's user=, group=, extra_groups=[], umask= and process_group= instead. The bootstrap sets rlimits on itself before importing user code.

Optional: in both modes, seed the global `random` from rng_seed before make_controller, so naive controllers replay.

issues.problem:
Running as the same uid, a child can:
- `os.kill(os.getppid(), SIGKILL)` the host;
- `os.kill(-1, SIGKILL)`, killing every process the user owns on a dev machine;
- drop a .pth file into .venv site-packages, which runs in every later topple invocation, host included;
- edit the editable-installed python/topple/*.py;
- use the network and read ~/.ssh;
- on macOS, read the host's environment via sysctl KERN_PROCARGS2;
- rewrite the submission between hashing and import, so the recorded sha256 no longer matches the code that ran.

Separately, "default subprocess for files that are not built-in" is ambiguous: no built-in category is defined.

Composite actions run with the caller's token and secrets.

The design doesn't cover:
- input handling, which is a script-injection risk if inputs are interpolated into run steps;
- which trigger events are safe to use;
- macOS runners, which have no Docker;
- MJCF `<include>` and asset paths that point outside the repo.
